This directory is the held-out benchmark (GitLab #785). Nothing in it may be
mined into a training corpus, ever.

The marker is load-bearing, not decorative. `Train/script/leakage.py`'s
`corpus_files()` — the single enumerator every leakage check and the benchmark
tests go through — skips any directory containing a file named `.never-mine`,
and `leakage.assert_mineable()` raises when pipeline code names a file inside
one by hand. The data files also carry `.benchmark.` in their names, which
`corpus_files()` excludes wherever the file sits, so a copy taken out of this
directory is still excluded.

Why this exists: the 4,000-prompt `aro ask` evaluation was folded back into
training (2,679 good answers became `code_generation` pairs, 1,160 repaired bad
answers became feedback pairs), and `Train/eval_prompts.json` — the release
gate's own prompt set — shares templates with `Train/Material/`. The project's
two quoted figures, 75.5 % and 67 %, were therefore measured on prompts the
model had paraphrased answers for. There was no untouched benchmark left.

If you are here because you want more training data: generate it. The whole
value of this directory is that it has never been trained on, and that value is
destroyed permanently and silently the first time somebody makes an exception.
